nal series  this first full-length episode is about  how chinese censorship actually works  and why the regime does sometimes  tolerate dissent  watch it with the curiosity stream and  nebula bundle right now 42 off for  mother's day just 12 bucks a year  if you enjoy these videos sign up to get  50 percent more of them starting today  on the very first day of finance 101  students are taught about the principal  agent problem  capitalism it goes is all about  specialization it isn't efficient or  even possible for each of us to sow our  own clothes solder our own electronics  and be our own doctors  so we outsource each of these jobs to  someone more qualified  modern life in other words requires  delegation  but how do a company's shareholders  ensure its managers who they delegate  decision making to don't misuse their  money  replace shareholders with voters and  managers with politicians and you  understand why eliminating corruption is  a really hard problem  that's in a democracy but even the most  iron fisted dictator can't possibly make  much less implement every last decision  on their own  they too must delegate  say you're the leader of china  whether or not you have the people's  best interest in mind you also know your  legitimacy and therefore your power  depends on some level of competence  while nationalism and censorship afford  the communist party plenty of room for  failure at the end of the day your  continued rule requires food on the  table gas in the tank and at least some  occasional clean water  on a purely organizational level take a  second to appreciate just how hard a  coordination problem it is to convert  words at the top into the actions 99  layers below which result in more high  speed rail than every other country put  together and maintain 10 percent gdp  growth for years on end  now for the sake of simplicity let's  condense those hypothetical 99 layers  into just three  at the very top of the pyramid are a  tiny number of elite 