mbers of its parliament are given a  monthly allowance three times higher  than the median household income  and its prime minister is the world's  highest paid political leader earning  four times as much as the u.s president  the results are compelling no politician  has been charged with corruption in two  decades and the country is tied for  second place on the corruption  perception index  but of course this is easier said than  done  high public salaries are a luxury most  of the world simply can't afford  it's no coincidence that the countries  at the top of the list are also the  world's wealthiest  of the top 10 for example the poorest is  new zealand with a per capita gdp of 41  000  china on the other hand discovered a  much cheaper solution  one invisible to the corruption  perception index which lumps half a  dozen different things together under  this nebulous umbrella term  more interesting than how much  corruption is what kind  yun ng in her book china's gilded age  argues there are actually four distinct  types  when the rich world hears the word  corruption we imagine the one percent of  the one percent presidents prime  ministers and oligarchs colluding in  poorly lit rooms to siphon off public  funds for personal gain  yachts mansions and private jets  that's grand theft  conversely people from lower income  regions may not have to imagine they can  simply remember a cop soliciting bribes  this is known as petty theft  whether small or large theft is bad for  everyone but the thief  when your leader embezzles public funds  to buy rare leopard skin hats you  receive nothing  but there's another dimension exchanges  transactions in which the buyer receives  special treatment  if you need 20 permits to open a  restaurant you almost have to pay what's  called speed money to overcome the red  tape  this is better than mere theft because  at least you get something in return  you're not worse off after paying the  bribe  still you're certainly not celebrating  but then there's something called access  money exchanges that make both parties  much better off  now make no mistake all corruption is  bad  but bad to who when and how  if you are a business the theft kind is  just a loss what economists would call a  tax  access money on the other hand is  actually an investment  suddenly you're able to buy certain  privileges that you previously couldn't  and this kind is precisely what china  has the most of  here's how it works  at the top of each regional government  remember is a powerful elite whose  performance is evaluated based on  objective metrics like economic growth  the problem his or her next promotion  requires the cooperation of hundreds or  thousands of poorly paid subordinates  what motivation does the bureaucrat  making 80 bucks a month have to play  along  so the two reach an agreement help me  climb the ladder and i'll make sure  you're well taken care of  government departments are allowed to  operate almost like independent  businesses keeping some portion of  whatever they collect in fees and taxes  some of this money in turn is used to  shower workers with perks that would  make even a google employee jealous  everything from food to vacations  parties housing electricity gas child  care furniture and equipment  although public wages are set below  market rates formal salaries only  comprise 24 of the average bureaucrats  total compensation  when these unofficial benefits are  included you can see they're actually  doing quite well  bureaucrats may be short on cash but  that doesn't mean you won't spot them on  the golf course or beach sipping  champagne  but don't lose the forest for the trees  the point is not that government workers  are well paid it's that they receive a  kind of unofficial commission  every last cadre no matter how low on  the org chart has a personal stake in  the country's development  does your office need new computers is  your family due for a tropical vacation  chin